\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{The first Wick powers}
\date{2026-11-28}

\p{The [Wick recursion](fdip-01CV) gives
##{\begin{aligned}
W_0(z;s)&=1,& W_1(z;s)&=z,\\
W_2(z;s)&=z^2-s,& W_3(z;s)&=z^3-3sz,\\
W_4(z;s)&=z^4-6sz^2+3s^2.
\end{aligned}}
In particular, for a real centered Gaussian #{Z} of variance #{s},
\ref{fdip-000B} and \ref{fdip-009B} give
#{\mathbb E W_2(Z;s)=0} and #{\mathbb E W_4(Z;s)=0}.}

\p{An interaction #{\nu W_2(z;s)} adds #{\nu z^2} to the action,
up to a constant. Since the Gaussian action is half the precision
quadratic form, this changes the precision by #{2\nu\bbI} when
summed over sites. The variance subtraction changes the normalizing
constant, while the quadratic coefficient changes the Gaussian
probability.}
